1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,235
Imagine climbing into an aircraft,

2
00:00:02,319 --> 00:00:04,756
preparing to fly across
the Atlantic Ocean,

3
00:00:04,839 --> 00:00:09,000
and discovering that the one thing
every pilot depends on is missing.

4
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:10,855
There is no front window.

5
00:00:10,939 --> 00:00:12,615
You cannot look straight ahead.

6
00:00:12,699 --> 00:00:15,236
You cannot see the runway
directly in front of you.

7
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:16,795
And once you take off,

8
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:20,920
thousands of miles of open ocean lie
between you and your destination.

9
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:23,356
Yet, in nineteen twenty-seven,

10
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:26,736
one man climbed into an
aircraft built exactly this way

11
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:30,480
and attempted one of the most
dangerous flights in aviation history.

12
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:33,460
The aircraft was called
the Spirit of St. Louis.

13
00:00:33,799 --> 00:00:35,695
Its pilot, Charles Lindbergh,

14
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:40,200
was about to attempt the first solo
nonstop flight from New York to Paris.

15
00:00:40,399 --> 00:00:43,439
But here is what makes the
aircraft so extraordinary.

16
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:46,396
The missing front window
was not an accident,

17
00:00:46,479 --> 00:00:48,095
a manufacturing mistake,

18
00:00:48,179 --> 00:00:49,875
or an unfinished design.

19
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:51,920
It was deliberately eliminated.

20
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:54,396
Instead of a conventional windshield,

21
00:00:54,479 --> 00:00:56,956
Lindbergh had to rely on a small periscope

22
00:00:57,039 --> 00:01:01,159
and carefully controlled movements of
the aircraft to see what was ahead.

23
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:05,555
Why would anyone design a plane that
made one of the pilot's most basic tasks

24
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:06,756
so difficult?

25
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:10,956
And how could a man fly for more than
thirty-three hours across an ocean

26
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:13,659
in an aircraft he could barely see out of?

27
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:17,135
The answer begins with a decision
that changed the entire design

28
00:01:17,219 --> 00:01:19,079
of the Spirit of St. Louis.

29
00:01:19,739 --> 00:01:23,760
The most unusual cockpit
in aviation history.

30
00:01:23,959 --> 00:01:25,156
At first glance,

31
00:01:25,239 --> 00:01:26,075
the Spirit of

32
00:01:26,159 --> 00:01:30,075
St. Louis looked like a fairly
conventional single-engine aircraft.

33
00:01:30,159 --> 00:01:32,036
It had wings, a tail,

34
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:32,995
a propeller,

35
00:01:33,079 --> 00:01:35,939
and a cockpit positioned
behind the engine.

36
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:40,019
But its most important difference
was hidden inside its nose.

37
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:42,676
Unlike conventional aircraft,

38
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:46,876
where the pilot sat behind the engine
with a forward windshield, the Spirit of

39
00:01:46,959 --> 00:01:51,640
St. Louis had a massive fuel tank occupying
the space directly ahead of Lindbergh.

40
00:01:51,799 --> 00:01:53,316
When he sat in the cockpit,

41
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:56,635
he could not look straight ahead
through the nose of the aircraft.

42
00:01:56,719 --> 00:01:57,715
His forward view was

43
00:01:57,799 --> 00:02:01,500
blocked by the very thing that made
the transatlantic journey possible:

44
00:02:01,719 --> 00:02:02,560
fuel.

45
00:02:02,719 --> 00:02:05,156
The aircraft needed to
carry enough fuel to travel

46
00:02:05,239 --> 00:02:07,355
thousands of miles without landing.

47
00:02:07,439 --> 00:02:10,475
There were no convenient
refueling stops over the Atlantic,

48
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:14,180
and Lindbergh could not afford to run
out of fuel before reaching Europe.

49
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:18,795
The solution was to build the aircraft
around an enormous fuel supply.

50
00:02:18,879 --> 00:02:22,195
The main tank was positioned
between the engine and the cockpit

51
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:24,096
rather than behind the pilot.

52
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:28,015
This arrangement created space for the
fuel needed for the journey and helped

53
00:02:28,099 --> 00:02:31,819
keep the aircraft's weight distribution
within the design requirements.

54
00:02:31,919 --> 00:02:35,060
But it also created an
extraordinary problem.

55
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:38,836
The pilot could no longer rely
on a normal forward-facing window

56
00:02:38,919 --> 00:02:40,556
to see where he was going.

57
00:02:40,639 --> 00:02:41,816
For most aircraft,

58
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:44,560
this would have been an
unacceptable compromise.

59
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:47,996
A pilot needs to see the runway
during takeoff and landing,

60
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:49,876
monitor the aircraft's position,

61
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:52,620
and keep track of other
aircraft in the sky.

62
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:57,120
Yet Lindbergh accepted a cockpit that
made all of these tasks more difficult.

63
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:00,580
The designers had not forgotten
the importance of visibility.

64
00:03:00,759 --> 00:03:03,439
They had simply found
another way to provide it,

65
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:07,960
and that solution would become one of the
most unusual features of the aircraft.

66
00:03:08,159 --> 00:03:11,240
The race that created an
extraordinary aircraft.

67
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:14,735
To understand why anyone would
build an aircraft like this,

68
00:03:14,819 --> 00:03:17,620
we have to go back to the
challenge that inspired it.

69
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:19,015
In the 1920s,

70
00:03:19,099 --> 00:03:20,876
aviation was still young.

71
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:23,235
Flying across oceans was dangerous.

72
00:03:23,319 --> 00:03:25,816
Aircraft technology
was developing rapidly,

73
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:29,400
and pilots were pushing the limits
of what machines could achieve.

74
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:33,360
But one challenge captured the
imagination of the aviation world.

75
00:03:33,919 --> 00:03:37,855
A wealthy businessman named Raymond
Orteig had offered a prize of twenty-five

76
00:03:37,939 --> 00:03:43,240
thousand dollars to the first aviator to
fly nonstop between New York and Paris.

77
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:46,000
It was an enormous amount
of money at the time.

78
00:03:46,419 --> 00:03:50,419
Several pilots and aviation teams
began preparing for the attempt.

79
00:03:50,659 --> 00:03:54,675
Some believed the prize could be won
with a carefully modified aircraft.

80
00:03:54,759 --> 00:03:58,819
Others were willing to invest
heavily in a completely new design.

81
00:03:59,039 --> 00:04:02,599
The challenge was not simply
about building a fast aircraft.

82
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:06,015
The plane had to carry enough
fuel to cross the Atlantic,

83
00:04:06,099 --> 00:04:08,756
remain stable over a very long flight,

84
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:12,340
and give its pilot a reasonable
chance of surviving the journey.

85
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:14,819
Charles Lindbergh believed he could win,

86
00:04:14,919 --> 00:04:18,336
but he did not have the financial
resources of the wealthiest aviation

87
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:19,319
teams.

88
00:04:19,699 --> 00:04:23,939
He needed a company willing to build an
aircraft specifically for his attempt.

89
00:04:24,279 --> 00:04:28,879
He eventually approached the Ryan Airlines
Corporation in San Diego, California.

90
00:04:29,079 --> 00:04:33,276
The company agreed to build the aircraft,
and its chief engineer, Donald Hall,

91
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:35,899
began working with
Lindbergh on the design.

92
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:38,735
The result was based on the Ryan M-2,

93
00:04:38,819 --> 00:04:42,100
but it was heavily modified
for the transatlantic mission.

94
00:04:42,379 --> 00:04:44,875
Every major decision had one purpose:

95
00:04:44,959 --> 00:04:49,899
maximize the aircraft's ability to
carry fuel and fly as far as possible.

96
00:04:50,459 --> 00:04:52,276
That meant sacrificing features

97
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:55,615
that might have been considered
essential on an ordinary aircraft.

98
00:04:55,699 --> 00:04:57,735
The cockpit was moved farther back.

99
00:04:57,819 --> 00:05:00,115
The fuel tank was placed
ahead of the pilot.

100
00:05:00,199 --> 00:05:04,220
The aircraft was designed around
endurance rather than passenger comfort.

101
00:05:04,620 --> 00:05:07,459
It was not being built
to make flying pleasant.

102
00:05:07,639 --> 00:05:09,920
It was being built to cross an ocean,

103
00:05:10,120 --> 00:05:13,920
and that mission would determine
almost everything about its design.

104
00:05:14,079 --> 00:05:16,675
Why the fuel tank had to go in front.

105
00:05:16,759 --> 00:05:20,420
The biggest challenge facing
the designers was fuel capacity.

106
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:25,536
The distance between New York and Paris
was enormous for an aircraft of the 1920s.

107
00:05:25,620 --> 00:05:28,935
Lindbergh needed enough fuel
not only to cover the distance,

108
00:05:29,019 --> 00:05:30,995
but also to deal with headwinds,

109
00:05:31,079 --> 00:05:32,016
changing weather,

110
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:33,435
navigation errors,

111
00:05:33,519 --> 00:05:35,720
and other unexpected conditions.

112
00:05:36,019 --> 00:05:40,480
The aircraft was designed to carry roughly
four hundred fifty gallons of fuel.

113
00:05:40,579 --> 00:05:44,675
That was an extraordinary amount for
a single-engine aircraft of its time.

114
00:05:44,759 --> 00:05:48,675
But carrying that much fuel created
a serious engineering problem.

115
00:05:48,759 --> 00:05:50,560
Where could the designers put it?

116
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:51,836
A large tank behind

117
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:55,435
the cockpit would have affected
the aircraft's weight distribution.

118
00:05:55,519 --> 00:05:59,115
Placing fuel in different
sections of the wings and fuselage

119
00:05:59,199 --> 00:06:03,639
could complicate the structure and the
way fuel was managed during the flight.

120
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:08,355
The designers instead placed the main
tank directly ahead of the cockpit.

121
00:06:08,439 --> 00:06:11,255
This helped concentrate the
fuel load in a location chosen

122
00:06:11,339 --> 00:06:14,096
for the aircraft's balance
and structural design.

123
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:17,935
It also meant the tank occupied the space
where a conventional forward windshield

124
00:06:18,019 --> 00:06:20,439
and direct line of
sight would normally be.

125
00:06:20,579 --> 00:06:22,379
There was another consideration.

126
00:06:22,639 --> 00:06:26,596
Lindbergh wanted the large fuel
tank between himself and the engine.

127
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:29,415
In the event of a serious
frontal crash, the tank

128
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:33,279
could provide a degree of separation
between the pilot and the engine.

129
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:36,459
But this decision came
with a major disadvantage.

130
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:39,495
The pilot could no longer
see directly ahead.

131
00:06:39,579 --> 00:06:43,196
The designers had effectively placed
the aircraft's most important resource

132
00:06:43,279 --> 00:06:47,115
in the position where the pilot would
normally expect to look through a window.

133
00:06:47,199 --> 00:06:49,156
And once that decision had been made,

134
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:52,220
the rest of the cockpit had
to be designed around it.

135
00:06:53,079 --> 00:06:56,276
The question was no longer whether
Lindbergh would have a normal forward

136
00:06:56,360 --> 00:06:57,194
view.

137
00:06:57,339 --> 00:07:00,019
It was how he could
fly safely without one.

138
00:07:00,160 --> 00:07:02,939
How do you fly an aircraft
you cannot see through?

139
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:06,819
The Spirit of St. Louis did not
leave Lindbergh completely blind.

140
00:07:07,079 --> 00:07:09,315
The aircraft had windows on the sides,

141
00:07:09,399 --> 00:07:12,199
allowing him to see parts
of the surrounding sky.

142
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:15,536
But those windows could not provide
the same forward view that pilots

143
00:07:15,620 --> 00:07:16,980
were accustomed to.

144
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:20,279
So the designers introduced
an unusual solution.

145
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:23,815
A small periscope was installed
on the left side of the cockpit.

146
00:07:23,899 --> 00:07:27,675
It allowed Lindbergh to see forward
by using mirrors to redirect the view

147
00:07:27,759 --> 00:07:30,699
from outside the aircraft
into his line of sight.

148
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:33,795
It was not the same as looking
through a normal windshield.

149
00:07:33,879 --> 00:07:37,935
The image was limited, and the device
required the pilot to adjust his position

150
00:07:38,019 --> 00:07:40,180
and attention to use it effectively.

151
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:42,899
Lindbergh also had another technique.

152
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,295
When he needed to look forward or
inspect something directly ahead,

153
00:07:46,379 --> 00:07:50,079
he could carefully bank the aircraft
and look through the side windows.

154
00:07:50,199 --> 00:07:52,016
By changing the aircraft's angle,

155
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:55,019
he could bring parts of
the area ahead into view.

156
00:07:55,519 --> 00:07:57,696
This was not an ideal arrangement,

157
00:07:57,779 --> 00:07:59,995
especially during takeoff and landing.

158
00:08:00,079 --> 00:08:01,415
It required skill,

159
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:02,636
concentration,

160
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:07,120
and a thorough understanding of how the
aircraft responded to control inputs.

161
00:08:07,259 --> 00:08:09,699
But Lindbergh was an experienced pilot.

162
00:08:10,019 --> 00:08:13,956
He understood that the aircraft had
been built for a very specific mission,

163
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:18,319
and he was prepared to adapt his flying
technique to its unusual cockpit.

164
00:08:18,420 --> 00:08:21,615
The design also placed a
heavy burden on the pilot.

165
00:08:21,699 --> 00:08:24,660
He could not simply sit
back and enjoy the journey.

166
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:27,076
He had to monitor the instruments,

167
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:28,415
manage the engine,

168
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:29,755
maintain his course,

169
00:08:29,839 --> 00:08:33,299
and constantly make decisions
about the aircraft's position.

170
00:08:33,580 --> 00:08:36,296
The periscope helped solve
the visibility problem,

171
00:08:36,379 --> 00:08:38,779
but it did not make the
cockpit comfortable,

172
00:08:38,899 --> 00:08:41,279
and comfort was never the main priority.

173
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:45,696
The aircraft had been designed to
carry enough fuel to reach Paris.

174
00:08:45,779 --> 00:08:48,419
Everything else had to
work around that objective.

175
00:08:48,679 --> 00:08:50,975
The aircraft built around one number,

176
00:08:51,059 --> 00:08:52,779
thirty-six hundred miles.

177
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:57,320
The Spirit of St. Louis was not designed
to be the fastest aircraft in the sky.

178
00:08:57,639 --> 00:09:01,820
Its mission was to fly farther than
most aircraft of its time could manage.

179
00:09:02,039 --> 00:09:05,596
That meant its designers had to pay
attention to every pound of weight

180
00:09:05,679 --> 00:09:07,419
and every gallon of fuel.

181
00:09:07,559 --> 00:09:11,159
The aircraft had a wingspan of
approximately forty-six feet.

182
00:09:11,659 --> 00:09:15,655
It was powered by a Wright
J5C Whirlwind radial engine,

183
00:09:15,740 --> 00:09:18,659
producing around two hundred
and twenty-three horsepower.

184
00:09:19,019 --> 00:09:23,196
For an aircraft expected to carry
such a heavy fuel load over an ocean,

185
00:09:23,279 --> 00:09:26,019
that engine was a crucial
part of the design.

186
00:09:26,159 --> 00:09:30,519
It had to provide reliable power for a
journey lasting more than a full day.

187
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:35,515
The aircraft's empty weight was roughly
two thousand one hundred fifty pounds.

188
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:37,296
But once filled with fuel,

189
00:09:37,379 --> 00:09:40,895
carrying Lindbergh and all the
equipment required for the flight,

190
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:42,255
its takeoff weight rose

191
00:09:42,340 --> 00:09:46,080
to approximately five thousand
one hundred thirty-five pounds.

192
00:09:46,379 --> 00:09:49,139
That was a tremendous
load for the aircraft.

193
00:09:49,279 --> 00:09:52,580
The fuel alone accounted for
a major part of its weight.

194
00:09:52,873 --> 00:09:54,629
The designers had to make the wings,

195
00:09:54,713 --> 00:09:57,189
fuselage, landing gear,
and other components

196
00:09:57,273 --> 00:10:01,133
strong enough to handle the aircraft
while avoiding unnecessary weight.

197
00:10:01,273 --> 00:10:03,373
Every additional pound mattered.

198
00:10:03,473 --> 00:10:06,569
A heavier aircraft needed
more lift and more power.

199
00:10:06,653 --> 00:10:09,352
More power could mean
greater fuel consumption.

200
00:10:09,513 --> 00:10:13,593
Greater fuel consumption could reduce
the distance the aircraft could travel.

201
00:10:13,753 --> 00:10:15,573
It was a chain reaction.

202
00:10:15,773 --> 00:10:19,129
The design therefore became
an exercise in compromise.

203
00:10:19,213 --> 00:10:23,009
The aircraft needed enough strength
to carry its enormous fuel load,

204
00:10:23,093 --> 00:10:26,393
but it also had to remain light
enough to fly efficiently.

205
00:10:26,813 --> 00:10:31,469
The Spirit of St. Louis was not simply
a plane with a large tank added to it.

206
00:10:31,553 --> 00:10:32,469
It was an aircraft

207
00:10:32,553 --> 00:10:36,553
engineered around the challenge of
carrying that tank across the Atlantic.

208
00:10:36,693 --> 00:10:38,849
And even after all the engineering work,

209
00:10:38,933 --> 00:10:42,233
there was one factor the
designers could not fully control,

210
00:10:42,633 --> 00:10:45,313
the human being sitting
inside the cockpit.

211
00:10:45,473 --> 00:10:47,893
The real enemy was human endurance.

212
00:10:48,093 --> 00:10:50,813
The Spirit of St. Louis
could carry the fuel.

213
00:10:50,973 --> 00:10:53,029
Its engine could produce the power.

214
00:10:53,113 --> 00:10:55,293
Its wings could generate the lift.

215
00:10:55,753 --> 00:10:59,213
But none of that guaranteed
that the flight would succeed.

216
00:10:59,333 --> 00:11:03,189
The aircraft had to remain in the
air for more than thirty hours,

217
00:11:03,273 --> 00:11:08,093
and Lindbergh would have to remain awake
and alert for almost the entire journey.

218
00:11:08,313 --> 00:11:10,213
That was a serious challenge.

219
00:11:11,093 --> 00:11:14,069
Flying in the 1920s was already demanding.

220
00:11:14,153 --> 00:11:17,569
Pilots had to monitor their
instruments, adjust the controls,

221
00:11:17,653 --> 00:11:19,209
manage engine performance,

222
00:11:19,293 --> 00:11:22,253
and navigate using
relatively basic equipment.

223
00:11:22,493 --> 00:11:25,329
Now imagine doing all of
that for more than a day

224
00:11:25,413 --> 00:11:27,873
without a proper opportunity to rest.

225
00:11:28,313 --> 00:11:31,349
Lindbergh had limited
room inside the cockpit.

226
00:11:31,433 --> 00:11:33,569
The seat was not designed for comfort,

227
00:11:33,653 --> 00:11:37,069
and the aircraft did not have the
facilities of a modern long-distance

228
00:11:37,153 --> 00:11:38,453
passenger plane.

229
00:11:38,553 --> 00:11:42,013
There was no autopilot to take
over the controls while he slept.

230
00:11:42,293 --> 00:11:45,069
He had to remain focused
while dealing with fatigue,

231
00:11:45,153 --> 00:11:46,129
changing weather,

232
00:11:46,213 --> 00:11:48,333
and the possibility of engine trouble.

233
00:11:48,453 --> 00:11:51,049
The aircraft also carried
no second pilot who could

234
00:11:51,133 --> 00:11:53,529
take over if Lindbergh became exhausted.

235
00:11:53,613 --> 00:11:54,773
He was alone.

236
00:11:55,173 --> 00:11:58,949
And the lack of a forward window made
the workload even more demanding.

237
00:11:59,033 --> 00:12:00,869
He had to use the periscope and side

238
00:12:00,953 --> 00:12:03,309
windows when he needed
to check what lay ahead

239
00:12:03,393 --> 00:12:06,293
while continuing to monitor
the aircraft's instruments.

240
00:12:06,533 --> 00:12:09,553
There was also the constant
concern of fuel management.

241
00:12:10,173 --> 00:12:12,929
If the engine consumed
more fuel than expected,

242
00:12:13,013 --> 00:12:15,453
the aircraft might not reach Paris.

243
00:12:15,573 --> 00:12:17,529
If the weather forced him off course,

244
00:12:17,613 --> 00:12:19,393
the distance could increase.

245
00:12:19,613 --> 00:12:23,533
A successful flight required more
than a well-designed aircraft.

246
00:12:24,353 --> 00:12:27,249
It required a pilot capable
of enduring conditions that

247
00:12:27,333 --> 00:12:29,213
few people had experienced.

248
00:12:29,313 --> 00:12:31,969
And as the aircraft
prepared to leave New York,

249
00:12:32,053 --> 00:12:35,893
all the engineering calculations were
about to face their biggest test.

250
00:12:36,293 --> 00:12:38,533
The flight that proved the design worked.

251
00:12:38,733 --> 00:12:43,269
On May 20th, 1927, Charles
Lindbergh climbed into the Spirit of

252
00:12:43,353 --> 00:12:46,153
St. Louis at Roosevelt Field in New York.

253
00:12:46,273 --> 00:12:48,793
The aircraft was heavily loaded with fuel.

254
00:12:48,893 --> 00:12:51,133
Its weight made the takeoff difficult.

255
00:12:51,313 --> 00:12:53,709
The runway was not particularly long,

256
00:12:53,793 --> 00:12:57,553
and the aircraft needed to build
enough speed to lift off safely.

257
00:12:57,753 --> 00:12:59,009
As it accelerated,

258
00:12:59,093 --> 00:13:02,053
the landing gear bounced
over the uneven surface.

259
00:13:02,173 --> 00:13:03,029
For a moment,

260
00:13:03,113 --> 00:13:05,133
the aircraft appeared to struggle,

261
00:13:05,253 --> 00:13:07,473
but it finally lifted into the air.

262
00:13:07,693 --> 00:13:09,489
Lindbergh was on his way.

263
00:13:09,573 --> 00:13:11,429
His destination was Paris,

264
00:13:11,513 --> 00:13:13,973
more than thirty-six hundred miles away.

265
00:13:14,093 --> 00:13:17,489
The first part of the flight was
only the beginning of the challenge.

266
00:13:17,573 --> 00:13:20,148
He still had to cross the United States,

267
00:13:20,232 --> 00:13:21,489
reach the Atlantic,

268
00:13:21,573 --> 00:13:25,753
and navigate across an ocean
with no convenient place to land.

269
00:13:25,933 --> 00:13:27,309
As the hours passed,

270
00:13:27,393 --> 00:13:29,773
fatigue became a constant threat.

271
00:13:30,053 --> 00:13:34,009
He encountered changing weather
and periods of poor visibility.

272
00:13:34,093 --> 00:13:38,673
He had to stay alert while managing the
aircraft and maintaining his course.

273
00:13:38,893 --> 00:13:41,093
The cockpit offered little comfort.

274
00:13:41,513 --> 00:13:43,109
There was no passenger cabin,

275
00:13:43,193 --> 00:13:44,429
no second pilot,

276
00:13:44,513 --> 00:13:47,493
and no opportunity to step
away from the controls.

277
00:13:47,713 --> 00:13:51,768
The aircraft's unusual design was now
being tested in the exact conditions

278
00:13:51,852 --> 00:13:53,432
it had been created for.

279
00:13:53,613 --> 00:13:56,689
The large fuel tank gave
Lindbergh the range he needed.

280
00:13:56,773 --> 00:13:58,669
The engine continued to run.

281
00:13:58,753 --> 00:14:00,833
The aircraft remained in the air.

282
00:14:01,113 --> 00:14:04,209
After approximately thirty-three
hours and thirty minutes,

283
00:14:04,293 --> 00:14:07,149
Lindbergh finally reached
the European coast.

284
00:14:07,233 --> 00:14:09,113
He continued toward Paris.

285
00:14:09,293 --> 00:14:11,829
When the Spirit of St. Louis
approached the city,

286
00:14:11,913 --> 00:14:14,553
crowds gathered at Le Bourget Airfield.

287
00:14:14,713 --> 00:14:18,329
Thousands of people had come to
witness the arrival of an aircraft

288
00:14:18,413 --> 00:14:21,573
that had attempted something
many considered impossible.

289
00:14:21,753 --> 00:14:24,289
On May 21st, 1927,

290
00:14:24,373 --> 00:14:25,893
Lindbergh landed.

291
00:14:26,113 --> 00:14:29,409
He had flown approximately three
thousand six hundred and ten miles

292
00:14:29,493 --> 00:14:32,433
from New York to Paris without stopping.

293
00:14:32,913 --> 00:14:36,453
The flight lasted around thirty-three
hours and thirty minutes.

294
00:14:36,573 --> 00:14:38,389
He had won the Orteig Prize

295
00:14:38,473 --> 00:14:42,849
and become the first person to complete a
solo non-stop flight across the Atlantic

296
00:14:42,933 --> 00:14:44,653
between those two cities.

297
00:14:44,933 --> 00:14:47,569
The aircraft without a
conventional front window

298
00:14:47,653 --> 00:14:49,713
had done what it was designed to do.

299
00:14:50,053 --> 00:14:51,813
It had crossed the Atlantic,

300
00:14:51,913 --> 00:14:55,869
but the success of the flight did not mean
that the design was suitable for ordinary

301
00:14:55,953 --> 00:14:57,053
aviation.

302
00:14:57,213 --> 00:15:00,129
In fact, the very features
that made the Spirit of

303
00:15:00,213 --> 00:15:01,549
St. Louis successful

304
00:15:01,633 --> 00:15:04,673
also showed why it was
such an unusual aircraft.

305
00:15:05,033 --> 00:15:08,273
Why this design was never
meant to become normal.

306
00:15:08,513 --> 00:15:09,389
The Spirit of

307
00:15:09,473 --> 00:15:12,593
St. Louis was built for
one extraordinary purpose.

308
00:15:12,753 --> 00:15:15,693
It was not designed to carry
passengers comfortably,

309
00:15:15,813 --> 00:15:18,349
operate from ordinary airports every day,

310
00:15:18,433 --> 00:15:21,533
or become the blueprint for
future commercial aircraft.

311
00:15:21,653 --> 00:15:23,789
It was designed to complete a single

312
00:15:23,873 --> 00:15:25,913
extremely demanding flight.

313
00:15:26,033 --> 00:15:27,753
That distinction matters.

314
00:15:27,893 --> 00:15:29,629
The aircraft's large fuel tank was

315
00:15:29,713 --> 00:15:32,569
essential for the distance
Lindbergh needed to cover.

316
00:15:32,653 --> 00:15:36,989
Its position ahead of the cockpit helped
make the required fuel capacity possible,

317
00:15:37,073 --> 00:15:40,073
but it also created a
major visibility problem.

318
00:15:40,233 --> 00:15:42,109
For a long-distance record attempt,

319
00:15:42,193 --> 00:15:45,193
the designers were willing
to accept that compromise.

320
00:15:45,393 --> 00:15:46,969
For an everyday aircraft,

321
00:15:47,053 --> 00:15:49,293
it would have been much harder to justify.

322
00:15:49,533 --> 00:15:51,869
Pilots need a clear view during takeoff,

323
00:15:51,953 --> 00:15:53,449
landing, taxiing,

324
00:15:53,533 --> 00:15:55,389
and normal flight operations.

325
00:15:55,473 --> 00:15:58,849
A periscope and side window maneuvers
might work for a highly skilled

326
00:15:58,933 --> 00:16:01,989
pilot on a specialized aircraft,
but they were not a sensible

327
00:16:02,073 --> 00:16:06,253
replacement for a conventional windshield
across the wider aviation industry.

328
00:16:06,533 --> 00:16:10,489
The Spirit of St. Louis was also built
around the limitations of the technology

329
00:16:10,573 --> 00:16:12,613
available in the 1920s.

330
00:16:12,833 --> 00:16:17,333
Aircraft engines were less powerful
and less reliable than modern engines.

331
00:16:17,473 --> 00:16:19,589
Navigation equipment was basic.

332
00:16:19,673 --> 00:16:22,429
Long-distance flying
required careful planning,

333
00:16:22,513 --> 00:16:25,293
and fuel capacity was a major limitation.

334
00:16:25,753 --> 00:16:28,909
The aircraft's design
reflected those realities.

335
00:16:28,993 --> 00:16:32,349
It was an example of engineers
solving a specific problem

336
00:16:32,433 --> 00:16:35,513
by making a series of unusual compromises.

337
00:16:35,713 --> 00:16:37,489
And once the mission was complete,

338
00:16:37,573 --> 00:16:41,393
there was no reason for every
aircraft to copy those compromises.

339
00:16:42,113 --> 00:16:43,869
The Spirit of St. Louis became famous

340
00:16:43,953 --> 00:16:46,789
not because it introduced a
normal way to build aircraft,

341
00:16:46,873 --> 00:16:49,589
but because it showed how far
engineering could be pushed

342
00:16:49,673 --> 00:16:52,613
when a single objective mattered
more than everything else,

343
00:16:52,753 --> 00:16:55,733
which brings us back to the
question that started this story.

344
00:16:56,033 --> 00:16:58,273
Why was there no front window?

345
00:16:58,453 --> 00:17:01,149
The real reason there was no front window.

346
00:17:01,233 --> 00:17:02,069
The Spirit of

347
00:17:02,153 --> 00:17:04,769
St. Louis had no conventional front window

348
00:17:04,853 --> 00:17:05,909
because its designers

349
00:17:05,993 --> 00:17:11,053
needed to carry an enormous amount of fuel
for a nonstop flight across the Atlantic.

350
00:17:11,453 --> 00:17:14,629
The main fuel tank was placed
directly ahead of the cockpit,

351
00:17:14,713 --> 00:17:18,253
occupying the space where a normal
forward windshield would have been.

352
00:17:18,473 --> 00:17:22,009
That decision helped make the aircraft's
long-distance mission possible,

353
00:17:22,093 --> 00:17:25,593
but it left Charles Lindbergh without
a direct view through the nose.

354
00:17:25,913 --> 00:17:28,449
Instead, he used a small periscope

355
00:17:28,533 --> 00:17:31,049
and looked through the side
windows when necessary,

356
00:17:31,133 --> 00:17:34,213
sometimes banking the
aircraft to improve his view.

357
00:17:34,333 --> 00:17:37,533
It was an unusual solution
to an unusual problem,

358
00:17:37,653 --> 00:17:41,133
and it reveals something important
about the history of aviation.

359
00:17:41,573 --> 00:17:44,133
Engineering is often about compromise.

360
00:17:44,253 --> 00:17:47,849
A design that looks strange or
impractical in one situation

361
00:17:47,933 --> 00:17:50,413
may be exactly what is needed in another.

362
00:17:50,613 --> 00:17:54,329
The Spirit of St. Louis was not built
around the comfort of its pilot.

363
00:17:54,413 --> 00:17:56,853
It was not built to make flying easier.

364
00:17:56,973 --> 00:18:01,033
It was built to carry enough fuel to
cross an ocean in a single journey.

365
00:18:01,173 --> 00:18:02,929
And in May 1927,

366
00:18:03,013 --> 00:18:04,193
it succeeded.

367
00:18:04,433 --> 00:18:07,549
Charles Lindbergh flew for
more than thirty-three hours,

368
00:18:07,633 --> 00:18:11,089
covering approximately three
thousand six hundred and ten miles,

369
00:18:11,173 --> 00:18:12,793
and landed in Paris.

370
00:18:12,953 --> 00:18:16,113
The aircraft's missing front
window was not a mistake.

371
00:18:16,613 --> 00:18:19,509
It was the result of a
deliberate engineering decision

372
00:18:19,593 --> 00:18:23,633
made to solve a problem that seemed
almost impossible at the time.

373
00:18:23,893 --> 00:18:24,789
Sometimes,

374
00:18:24,873 --> 00:18:26,449
the most extraordinary aircraft

375
00:18:26,533 --> 00:18:29,333
are not the ones with the
most advanced technology.

376
00:18:29,873 --> 00:18:32,629
They are the ones whose
designers are willing to rethink

377
00:18:32,713 --> 00:18:36,329
what an aircraft needs to look
like in order to achieve something

378
00:18:36,413 --> 00:18:38,213
nobody has done before.

379
00:18:38,473 --> 00:18:42,089
And the Spirit of St. Louis proved
just how far that kind of thinking

380
00:18:42,173 --> 00:18:43,499
could take a plane.
